WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.500
File names and paths are untrusted input when a user, configuration file, archive, API request, or environment variable can influence them.

2
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:23.320
Path traversal occurs when input can escape an intended directory, often through dot-dot segments, absolute paths, platform-specific separators, symbolic links, or encoded path components.

3
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:29.660
A string that looks safe before normalization may resolve somewhere else after the operating system interprets it.

4
00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.740
Path handling should start with a clear policy.

5
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:40.680
Decide which base directory is allowed, whether relative paths are permitted, how canonicalization is performed, and how symbolic links are handled.

6
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.940
Extension checks are not enough because names can lie.

7
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:51.760
If file content matters, validate the actual content, expected header, format, and parser result.

8
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:59.460
Enforce limits on the bytes actually read, and handle errors without exposing sensitive internal paths or operational details.

9
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.840
Canonicalizing a pathname and opening it later is not race-safe in a writable directory.

10
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:16.460
The code example instead selects bounded content from a reviewed configuration snapshot; live workspace access needs a separate OS-enforced boundary, including protection against link substitution.

11
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:23.940
Structured formats such as JSON, CSV, XML, and binary records need schema and consistency checks.

12
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.040
Required fields may be missing.

13
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.460
Optional fields may appear in combinations the program does not support.

14
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.680
Version fields may select different parsing rules.

15
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:39.780
Lengths, counts, offsets, and nested structures can be inconsistent with the actual data available.

16
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:52.920
Defensive parsing checks structure before use, validates field relationships, limits nesting and record counts, and rejects malformed or surprising input before it influences memory, files, or control flow.
